# Line endings.
#
# Every text file is checked out with LF on every platform, whatever
# core.autocrlf says. Reasons:
#   - shell scripts (bootstrap.sh, test_all.sh, */run*.sh, the launchers in
#     ports/*/bin) do not run with CRLF
#   - audit logs, tokens and canonical JSON are byte formats; fixtures and
#     vectors must be the same bytes on Windows as on Linux
#   - the bundle version and signature normalise CRLF to LF themselves
#     (spec/PORTING.md, "Bundle"), so a CRLF bundle still loads and verifies in
#     every engine; `python scripts/check_crlf.py` proves that. With this file
#     that tolerance is not needed for a checkout, only for files someone
#     edits or copies with a Windows tool.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows command files need CRLF: cmd.exe misreads labels and multi-line
# blocks in a batch file with bare LF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# PowerShell reads both. LF keeps the files identical on the machines that
# run them with PowerShell 7 on Linux and macOS (CI) and on Windows.
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# Never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.jar binary
*.jsa binary
*.class binary
*.whl binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.zip binary
*.phar binary
*.exe binary
*.so binary
*.dll binary
*.dylib binary

# Scripts without an extension.
ports/java/bin/smartdelegate-java text eol=lf
ports/php/bin/smartdelegate-php text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
